19
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:17,877
The Morioka Prosecutor's Office indicted
Code Owe, who lived near the crime scene.

20
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:20,680
He was charged with
felony murder and robbery.

21
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:24,269
The events leading to
his arrest were thus.

22
00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:28,657
While being questioned by three officers
on patrol, Code Oe suddenly fled.

23
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:30,858
The patrolman chased Oe to his home.

24
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:34,927
Once inside, the officers
discovered a knife covered in blood.

25
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:37,660
Oe was arrested in flagrante delicto.

26
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,140
After DNA analysis,

27
00:02:41,540 --> 00:02:45,658
the blood on the blade was revealed
to be a match to the victim's DNA.

28
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:47,090
O .A.

29
00:02:47,173 --> 00:02:51,337
consistently denied all charges Asked
to explain the knife, he insisted.

30
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:53,157
I just picked it up.

31
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:54,737
It wasn't mine.

32
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:57,467
A little hard to believe,
don't you think so?

33
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:00,017
He didn't flee questioning
because of his cat.

34
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:01,857
He fled because he was guilty.

35
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:03,077
I don't think so.

36
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:05,997
He had been wrongly detained
by the police before.

37
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:09,315
When an acquaintance of his
was busted for using drugs?

38
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:11,757
You're saying the police traumatized him?

39
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:17,060
But even so, I picked it up Who
picks up a bloody knife? No one.

40
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,157
He claims he was planning to
turn it in to the police later.

41
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:22,070
Later?

42
00:03:23,060 --> 00:03:24,520
I know, I know.

43
00:03:24,860 --> 00:03:26,757
His claims are all hard to believe.

44
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:29,017
But when you consider Owe's background,

45
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:32,697
the fact he works as a live-in
employee at a non-profit organization,

46
00:03:32,780 --> 00:03:35,486
it definitely starts
sounding plausible to me.

47
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:37,160
What kind of non-profit?

48
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:41,640
A shelter for the elderly
who have nowhere else to go.

49
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:44,457
They provide support for
their independent living.

50
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:47,357
Owe's job was taking care of
the residents who lived there.

51
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:50,017
However, they didn't pay
him a salary with wages.

52
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:53,041
They only supplied him
with goods, like groceries,

53
00:03:53,420 --> 00:03:57,302
boxed lunches, and gifts of pocket
money for New Year's sometimes.

54
00:03:57,460 --> 00:03:58,977
And if you could believe it,

55
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:02,766
they still charged him 50,000
yen a month for rent on his room.

56
00:04:03,260 --> 00:04:04,090
Brutal.

57
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:07,889
Apparently, quite a few of the
residents have prior records.

58
00:04:08,220 --> 00:04:11,617
Anyway, when asked why he didn't turn
in the knife as soon as he found it...

59
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:13,876
The rules say we can't call cop cars.

60
00:04:14,180 --> 00:04:18,415
or ambulances either they don't want
loud sirens bothering the neighbors

61
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:23,257
a non-profit sounds pretty suspicious yeah

62
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:26,100
it's extremely close to
being outright illegal

63
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:29,217
since the earthquake relief
funds started flowing into this

64
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:32,880
region we've seen an increase
in suspiciously managed npos

65
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:36,877
so it's possible the murder
weapon was just lying

66
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:40,400
there i think there's a
good chance that oa is

67
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:43,690
innocent.

68
00:04:43,773 --> 00:04:47,356
First, we'll investigate the
organization's activities and tax filings,

69
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:49,377
then run background checks
on all the residents.

70
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:51,897
I'll focus on getting more
information out of OA.

71
00:04:51,980 --> 00:04:54,037
You handle the investigation, Shimizu.

72
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:57,179
You're dumping a huge job
like that on me out of the

73
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:00,790
blue?

74
00:05:00,873 --> 00:05:02,857
And could you take care
of his cat? His cat?

75
00:05:02,940 --> 00:05:07,197
The commission on a public defender case
is zilch, no matter how many hours we bill.

76
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:09,477
And the public always
assumes they're guilty.

77
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:11,857
Plus, there's no suspended
sentence in murder cases,

78
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:13,940
so he could get the death penalty.

79
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:16,808
There's nothing to gain
by taking this case.

80
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:18,190
Higuruma,

81
00:05:18,273 --> 00:05:21,897
will you please explain to me why you
go around accepting cases like this one?

82
00:05:21,980 --> 00:05:23,509
It's good once in a while.

83
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:26,504
If we stuck to easy work,
our skills would dull.

84
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:28,444
It's more than once in a while.

85
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:30,737
It's more than once in a while!

86
00:05:30,820 --> 00:05:33,349
You're saying your
thoughts out loud again.

87
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:38,365
Sounds like Higuruma
hasn't changed a bit then.

88
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:43,757
It's tough working for an independent
lawyer who isn't in it for the money.

89
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:48,317
Yeah, well, at least I'm not stuck having
to run a cafe on the side like you are.

90
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:49,437
Hey, wait a minute.

91
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:53,017
When you say he hasn't changed, you
mean Higuruma's always been like this?

92
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:54,620
Hmm? Not really.

93
00:05:55,060 --> 00:05:57,589
I think he actually
used to be a lot worse.

94
00:05:58,060 --> 00:05:58,890
Huh.

95
00:05:59,220 --> 00:06:00,677
Back when he was with us,

96
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:04,760
he was in charge of a reckless
driving case that resulted in injury.

97
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:06,557
Specifically, it was for drunk driving.

98
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:08,800
The defendant was only 19,

99
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:11,357
and his co-workers had
coerced him into doing

100
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:14,200
both the drinking part
and the driving part.

101
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:17,597
However, those involved
all aligned their stories,

102
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:19,537
and he didn't have the
money for a settlement,

103
00:06:19,620 --> 00:06:22,267
and we couldn't get him
a suspended sentence.

104
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:30,157
You liar!

105
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:32,828
You promised me they
would find me innocent!

106
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:37,777
You promised to get him a suspended
sentence, not an innocent verdict,

107
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:40,377
although you didn't manage to
do either, so there's that.

108
00:06:40,460 --> 00:06:42,357
You know what the takeaway from this is?

109
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:45,517
Quit being a criminal defense
lawyer for impossible cases.

110
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:47,597
Trying to save the week
may be a noble endeavor,

111
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:49,977
but there's a limit to
what lawyers like us can do

112
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:52,737
without any cooperation from
the courts and prosecutors.

113
00:06:52,820 --> 00:06:55,177
They don't want to sit and
hear a client's sob story.

114
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:56,997
They just want to clear their dockets.

115
00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:00,315
It's not worth continuing
when your clients resent you.

116
00:07:01,820 --> 00:07:05,585
They're at the end of their rope,
both mentally and financially.

117
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:08,472
I can't blame them for lashing out at me.

118
00:07:10,820 --> 00:07:13,980
But Higuruma, what
about your mental state?

119
00:07:16,060 --> 00:07:16,890
Well, I'm...

120
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:42,580
We, the people, find
the defendant innocent.

121
00:07:43,180 --> 00:07:44,480
Innocent? What?

122
00:07:47,900 --> 00:07:50,240
He actually won this case?

123
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:53,260
Not yet.

124
00:07:54,920 --> 00:07:56,520
They'll appeal right away.

125
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:04,220
The prosecution is appealing.

126
00:08:13,180 --> 00:08:17,637
After the police searched your room for
stolen items, nothing was found there.

127
00:08:17,720 --> 00:08:19,817
And in the same non-profit residence,

128
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:23,157
another person went missing right
after the crime was committed.

129
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:26,257
Luckily, you were caught on the
security feet of a convenience store.

130
00:08:26,340 --> 00:08:28,837
That and the condition
of the corpses proved you

131
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:31,557
couldn't have been at
the scene of the murders.

132
00:08:31,640 --> 00:08:35,346
Boy, it's a good thing you don't
know how to cook for yourself.

133
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:36,670
Higuruma, sir.

134
00:08:36,753 --> 00:08:38,639
I want to thank you for everything.

135
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:41,217
There's no need to call me sir.

136
00:08:41,300 --> 00:08:42,888
We're not in the clear yet.

137
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:44,551
There's still the appeal.

138
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:47,273
That's not what I mean.

139
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:52,740
You're the only one who
believed me. Thank you.

140
00:09:02,140 --> 00:09:06,199
While defense lawyers have to work
with limited budgets and manpower,

141
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:11,337
prosecutors can use taxpayer money to pour
on the lawyers and dig up the evidence.

142
00:09:11,420 --> 00:09:12,400
That's fine.

143
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:14,149
It's how the system works.

144
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:19,577
However, the prosecutors didn't submit
any new evidence during the second trial.

145
00:09:19,660 --> 00:09:20,490
trial.

146
00:09:20,573 --> 00:09:22,777
The judge accepted their absurd claim that

147
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:25,389
there was no reason to
consider the missing

148
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:28,197
person as a suspect.

149
00:09:28,280 --> 00:09:29,937
So they overturned the verdict.

150
00:09:30,020 --> 00:09:33,608
The highest court is always the
bottleneck for a third trial.

151
00:09:33,720 --> 00:09:36,485
Most final appeals are
turned away at the door.

152
00:09:36,580 --> 00:09:37,521
It's challenging

153
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:40,557
to even get a proper trial there.

154
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:44,360
This trial was destined for a
guilty verdict from the start.

155
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:56,060
Why are you looking at me that way?

156
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:03,680
But Higuruma, what
about your mental state?

157
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:08,927
Well, I'm not trying to promote
the ideals of saving the weak.

158
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:11,657
It's just always gone
against my nature to leave

159
00:10:11,740 --> 00:10:14,117
things alone when I
feel like they're wrong.

160
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:16,788
I've just never been
able to fix that habit.

161
00:10:17,380 --> 00:10:21,577
The Goddess of Justice blinds her sight
to maintain fairness under the law,

162
00:10:21,660 --> 00:10:25,954
and people turn a blind eye to all
kinds of things to protect themselves.

163
00:10:27,060 --> 00:10:31,531
But I don't want to brush away the hands
of people who cling to me for help.

164
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:37,500
The least I can do is keep my eyes open.

165
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:43,517
If there are any objections?

166
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:44,797
To today's trial!

167
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:46,597
Submit your final appeal in writing.

168
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:47,957
Within 15 days.

169
00:10:48,040 --> 00:10:49,500
Which includes today?

170
00:11:01,340 --> 00:11:03,046
Higuruma, what are you doing?

171
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:07,920
Everyone come back.

172
00:11:12,860 --> 00:11:14,800
We're having a retrial!

173
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:19,757
Higuruma's in.

174
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:22,037
Shinjuku. Ikebukuro.

175
00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:23,897
Got it? Shinjuku.

176
00:11:23,980 --> 00:11:26,537
I hope Itadori's headed there too?

177
00:11:26,620 --> 00:11:28,140
Ikebukuro, huh?

178
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:30,445
I wonder if Fushiguro's there.

179
00:11:34,260 --> 00:11:39,177
Oh, isn't she running a little late
that girl better get here soon or else!

180
00:11:39,260 --> 00:11:43,848
But if she doesn't have my next sucker
with her, she'd better not come at all.

181
00:11:51,540 --> 00:11:56,700
What's your problem no no no
no we asked the questions.

182
00:11:56,880 --> 00:11:58,998
What are you doing around here, huh?

183
00:11:59,420 --> 00:12:00,760
This is getting old

184
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:04,720
Why are you keeping quiet?

185
00:12:04,980 --> 00:12:07,039
You're better off laughing with us.

186
00:12:11,480 --> 00:12:13,897
Oh dear, what did I tell you?

187
00:12:13,980 --> 00:12:17,840
Not that I actually told you,
but why can't you understand?

188
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:19,300
Are you an idiot?

189
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:24,193
These guys aren't that smart or athletic,
but they're brimming with pride.

190
00:12:24,660 --> 00:12:28,557
All you have to do is just match their
attitude and joke around with them,

191
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:30,597
so you don't hurt that ego of theirs.

192
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:34,537
Then you won't get targeted
by anyone else either and

193
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:38,037
you might even have a good
time now and then knock it

194
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:43,077
off hmm here comes another

195
00:12:43,160 --> 00:12:45,097
idiot is that uniform from

196
00:12:45,180 --> 00:12:49,200
West Middle you're a middle
school you need to learn some

197
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:51,030
respect.

198
00:12:51,113 --> 00:12:52,917
We're gonna teach you a lesson.

199
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:54,440
Down you go!

200
00:12:55,120 --> 00:12:55,950
Huh?

201
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:12,620
You really shouldn't be so violent!

202
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:29,930
And you?

203
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:35,340
Y-you mean me?

204
00:13:36,660 --> 00:13:37,490
Hey.

205
00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:38,820
What now?

206
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:42,977
You aren't planning to go through
Ikebukuro to reach Shinjuku, are you?

207
00:13:43,060 --> 00:13:44,917
That area is rich with supplies.

208
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,647
We're more likely to
encounter other players.

209
00:13:48,360 --> 00:13:50,577
There are two reasons for going this way.

210
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:54,560
The route is easy to follow, and
I happen to have a base nearby.

211
00:13:56,320 --> 00:13:58,260
I'm just dying to take a shower.

212
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:01,405
I haven't had a chance
to rest since yesterday.

213
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:06,894
Higuruma uses this theater as his base.

214
00:14:07,240 --> 00:14:09,240
Assuming he hasn't moved, that is.

215
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:10,410
Oh, cool.

216
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:12,037
You've been a big help, man.

217
00:14:12,120 --> 00:14:12,950
Thank you.

218
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:15,840
Uh, wait a minute. Hold up. Don't go!

219
00:14:16,860 --> 00:14:17,690
What is it?

220
00:14:17,900 --> 00:14:19,429
You're seriously going in?

221
00:14:19,520 --> 00:14:23,577
Well, I'd have preferred to go in
with my friend, but I'm in a hurry.

222
00:14:23,660 --> 00:14:25,957
I never actually met Higuruma myself,

223
00:14:26,040 --> 00:14:29,569
but I once saw him beat Haba
to a pulp right in front of me.

224
00:14:29,860 --> 00:14:31,389
Then I'll totally be okay.

225
00:14:31,540 --> 00:14:35,834
I beat Propeller Head too, which means
I'll at least be able to run away.

226
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:37,720
See you later.

227
00:14:40,420 --> 00:14:41,380
Tell him.

228
00:14:42,040 --> 00:14:43,740
Come on, man, tell him.

229
00:14:44,540 --> 00:14:46,380
You should stop doing this.

230
00:14:52,980 --> 00:14:56,020
I'm sorry, Hitotori.

231
00:14:58,800 --> 00:15:00,820
It's about time, Higuruma.

232
00:15:02,680 --> 00:15:05,820
Sorry, I guess we were
both expecting to see him.

233
00:15:06,120 --> 00:15:07,000
I'm Reggie.

234
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:09,240
And she deceived you.

235
00:15:12,820 --> 00:15:14,360
Don't give me that look.

236
00:15:14,760 --> 00:15:16,642
You don't scare me for a second.

237
00:15:16,860 --> 00:15:18,237
And just so you know, Reggie here...

238
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:19,150
Quiet!

239
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:27,500
I'm wasting my time here.

240
00:15:38,620 --> 00:15:39,640
Who's there?

241
00:15:40,020 --> 00:15:41,720
What are you doing here?

242
00:15:43,880 --> 00:15:45,340
I could ask you that.

243
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:52,220
Ever have the chance to take a bath
while still fully clothed? Never.

244
00:15:54,040 --> 00:15:57,275
Surprisingly, it feels better
than I expected it would.

245
00:16:00,380 --> 00:16:03,640
You know, back when I
was in elementary school,

246
00:16:04,020 --> 00:16:07,160
I loved our safety classes
on swimming and clothes.

247
00:16:08,620 --> 00:16:11,197
So many things have stopped
mattering to me lately,

248
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:15,980
so I've been challenging myself to try
things I assumed I shouldn't be doing.

249
00:16:16,680 --> 00:16:19,977
I've basically gone off the
rails after hitting my mid-30s.

250
00:16:20,060 --> 00:16:20,890
Funny, huh?

251
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:23,997
I guess it's kind of amusing.

252
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:25,460
You're Higuruma, right?

253
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:27,340
In the flesh.

254
00:16:27,760 --> 00:16:28,820
I'm here to talk.

255
00:16:29,640 --> 00:16:30,520
Hold on.

256
00:16:30,680 --> 00:16:32,033
Wait, wait, wait, wait.

257
00:16:32,220 --> 00:16:33,820
I happen to be a lawyer.

258
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:39,460
For 30 minutes, I charge 5,000 yen to
consult, so either pay up or shut up. Huh?

259
00:16:41,580 --> 00:16:42,580
I'm just kidding.

260
00:16:42,740 --> 00:16:46,269
I wanted to see what it feels
like to be a cutthroat lawyer.

261
00:16:48,040 --> 00:16:48,870
I see.

262
00:16:48,953 --> 00:16:50,060
What's this guy doing?

263
00:16:51,360 --> 00:16:53,889
You still have a hundred
points, don't you?

264
00:16:54,340 --> 00:16:56,977
He's not a past sorcerer
who's been incarnated.

265
00:16:57,060 --> 00:17:00,017
He's a modern sorcerer whose
technique was activated.

266
00:17:00,100 --> 00:17:01,220
We can talk it out.

267
00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:03,186
There's room for negotiation.

268
00:17:03,540 --> 00:17:06,246
Okay, you've passed the
test with fine covers.

269
00:17:06,740 --> 00:17:09,093
You've got more than enough, I can tell.

270
00:17:09,220 --> 00:17:11,337
That's quite the Shikigami you have there.

271
00:17:11,420 --> 00:17:12,697
I bet you're strong.

272
00:17:12,780 --> 00:17:16,277
If you were weak, I'd just kill
you as another average mark.

273
00:17:16,360 --> 00:17:17,301
But you're good.

274
00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:18,856
Let's work together.

275
00:17:18,940 --> 00:17:19,770
What gives?

276
00:17:19,853 --> 00:17:21,093
I didn't agree to this.

277
00:17:22,920 --> 00:17:24,273
What are you proposing?

278
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:28,548
How much do you actually know
so far about this culling game?

279
00:17:29,260 --> 00:17:30,320
It's a ritual.

280
00:17:30,780 --> 00:17:33,257
It uses the cursed energy
of the players inside the

281
00:17:33,340 --> 00:17:36,437
barriers to ferry the people
of Japan to the other side.

282
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:39,167
And then changes them
into something inhuman.

283
00:17:39,900 --> 00:17:42,194
Guess I should stay quiet about Tengen.

284
00:17:42,840 --> 00:17:43,670
Really?

285
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:45,740
Do you know Kenjaku, then?

286
00:17:47,080 --> 00:17:51,727
I knew there had to be at least one
sorcerer connected to Kenjaku in this game.

287
00:17:53,680 --> 00:17:56,760
So, to be frank, we need your help.

288
00:17:57,260 --> 00:17:59,201
We want to stop the culling game.

289
00:18:00,120 --> 00:18:00,950
Timeout.

290
00:18:01,033 --> 00:18:04,369
When I say stop the game, I
mean stop the obligated killing.

291
00:18:04,620 --> 00:18:09,177
If we could use your hundred points, we'd
be able to add a new rule to the game.

292
00:18:09,260 --> 00:18:11,025
Permit me to be frank as well.

293
00:18:11,800 --> 00:18:12,740
I refuse.

294
00:18:15,120 --> 00:18:17,520
Is that another joke? No.

295
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:21,976
I just sense huge potential in letting
the culling game continue as is.

296
00:18:22,280 --> 00:18:24,104
Sometimes the law is powerless.

297
00:18:25,060 --> 00:18:27,297
However, think about the
rules of this culling game.

298
00:18:27,380 --> 00:18:31,540
If the power I've been granted is
real, then those rules must be too.

299
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:36,657
I think all your talk about players' cursed
energy being used inside the barriers?

300
00:18:36,740 --> 00:18:39,200
It's probably just a bluff. A bluff?

301
00:18:39,800 --> 00:18:43,780
Well, less of a bluff and more,
like you're describing Plan B or C.

302
00:18:44,240 --> 00:18:45,820
Here's three reasons why.

303
00:18:46,400 --> 00:18:51,580
The number of players, the strength gap
between players, and the barrier's rules.

304
00:18:51,960 --> 00:18:56,120
If roughly 1,000 people were equally
split up between the 10 colonies,

305
00:18:56,380 --> 00:18:59,400
that would come out to
100 people per colony.

306
00:19:00,800 --> 00:19:04,565
However, among the players are
sources like Kashimo or Higuruma,

307
00:19:04,660 --> 00:19:06,778
who are much stronger than the rest.

308
00:19:07,220 --> 00:19:10,637
Those two alone have already
culled 60 sorcerers between them,

309
00:19:10,720 --> 00:19:14,977
and I bet you there's a sorcerer just
like them in each of the other colonies.

310
00:19:15,060 --> 00:19:17,277
So then the weak are weeded out early on.

311
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:20,377
Does that mean the culling
game ends as a stalemate?

312
00:19:20,460 --> 00:19:22,166
He's confirmed my suspicions.

313
00:19:22,660 --> 00:19:24,420
The colony's been too quiet.

314
00:19:25,480 --> 00:19:26,680
It already has.

315
00:19:26,980 --> 00:19:29,640
Well, at least it has here in Tokyo No. 1.

316
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:33,737
On top of that, there's the teleportation
when people enter the colonies.

317
00:19:33,820 --> 00:19:36,057
It serves to spread out
players as they enter the game,

318
00:19:36,140 --> 00:19:38,377
but its main goal is
likely to encourage an

319
00:19:38,460 --> 00:19:40,817
early second awakening
of modern sorcerers.

320
00:19:40,900 --> 00:19:43,877
There are some who die during
the teleportation, too.

321
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:46,837
Now, if he was really trying to
use the player's cursed energy,

322
00:19:46,920 --> 00:19:48,857
then he would probably
want the greatest number of

323
00:19:48,940 --> 00:19:51,517
sorcerers to fight for
the longest time possible.

324
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:54,117
What if people who broke
the rules were punished?

325
00:19:54,200 --> 00:19:56,697
Not with the need of
indictments, prosecutions,

326
00:19:56,780 --> 00:19:59,839
or arguments at trials, but
by pure physics instead.

327
00:20:00,120 --> 00:20:01,708
Wouldn't that be wonderful?

328
00:20:01,840 --> 00:20:04,077
I'll grant you there's issues
with the current rules,

329
00:20:04,160 --> 00:20:08,219
but I want to watch over the system
serving as a basis for this game.

330
00:20:08,380 --> 00:20:10,851
It'll ruin everything if it ends too soon.

331
00:20:11,240 --> 00:20:13,397
I'd especially love to
witness at least once the

332
00:20:13,480 --> 00:20:16,539
stripping of a technique
mentioned in Rules 2 and 8.

333
00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:20,257
And it's better for the rules if
being stripped means death, too.

334
00:20:20,340 --> 00:20:22,957
The Cullingame itself
is considered a ritual.

335
00:20:23,040 --> 00:20:26,452
If we take too long, every
person in this nation will die.

336
00:20:26,660 --> 00:20:27,601
That's not true.

337
00:20:27,840 --> 00:20:30,597
The Cullingame insists on
its own ongoing longevity.

338
00:20:30,680 --> 00:20:32,798
I don't really get that part either.

339
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:36,780
I'll rephrase it, then.

340
00:20:38,040 --> 00:20:40,980
Let me use your 100 points, Higuruma!

341
00:20:42,480 --> 00:20:46,340
Have you ever killed someone
who really ticked you off?

342
00:20:49,640 --> 00:20:51,360
Feels better than I expected.

343
00:20:52,460 --> 00:20:53,880
A hell of a lot better.

344
00:20:55,140 --> 00:20:55,970
A Shikigami?

345
00:20:56,720 --> 00:20:58,280
Higuruma has 100 points.

346
00:20:58,600 --> 00:21:00,917
He might have killed 20 sorcerers already.

347
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:02,857
I can't just recklessly jump in.

348
00:21:02,940 --> 00:21:07,520
I'll go on defense, but I'll be
ready to respond to any attack.

349
00:21:10,680 --> 00:21:11,880
Domain expansion.

350
00:21:13,020 --> 00:21:14,220
Deadly sentencing.

351
00:21:26,620 --> 00:21:30,267
The idea of a rule not hindering
the continuation of the game,

352
00:21:30,500 --> 00:21:32,618
it's just a cover for the real plan.

353
00:21:32,940 --> 00:21:34,240
Mark my words,

354
00:21:34,720 --> 00:21:36,277
when only the strong remain,

355
00:21:36,360 --> 00:21:39,417
Kenjaku is going to drop a
bomb on this competition,

356
00:21:39,500 --> 00:21:42,540
and the Cullingame will
fulfill its purpose.

357
00:22:05,940 --> 00:22:08,740
Thank you.

358
00:23:40,960 --> 00:23:42,480
Now, make your argument.

359
00:23:42,700 --> 00:23:43,817
What if I'm judged guilty?

360
00:23:43,900 --> 00:23:45,157
Judge man, please begin.

361
00:23:45,240 --> 00:23:46,070
You mean I can lie?

362
00:23:46,153 --> 00:23:47,437
You get one chance to make a statement.

363
00:23:47,520 --> 00:23:48,717
I can't control cursed energy!

364
00:23:48,800 --> 00:23:51,677
How can he hold his own against
me without cursed energy?

365
00:23:51,760 --> 00:23:52,897
Hey, what a retrial!

366
00:23:52,980 --> 00:23:53,960
One more time!
